      "thinking": "The real issue is that the cache is unreachable, so every request pays the ~160ms connection-attempt cost before falling back. The fallback logic is fine but there's no circuit breaker, so the shop keeps redialing a dead cache on every single request instead of short-circuiting after the first failure.\n\nThis should live in summary_cache.py, wrapping look_up with breaker logic while keeping the existing caller contract (account_page still calls cached_summary and catches CacheUnreachable). I still want to raise CacheUnreachable when short-circuited, but need to reword the message since \"connection refused\" wouldn't be accurate anymore \u2014 something like \"skipped, cache unreachable since...\" while making sure the existing test's host/port assertions still pass for genuine failures. I'll need module-level breaker state keyed by endpoint, a cooldown window, and a way to reset it in tests, plus an injectable time source so tests aren't flaky.\n\nWriting breaker logic...\n\nThe key test to satisfy is that after one unreachable response, a second call within the cooldown window skips look_up entirely \u2014 currently it would call look_up every time, so I need the short-circuit check before dialling, with a clear message naming the endpoint and how long until the next retry is allowed.\n\nOn success I clear the failure record; on failure I stamp the cooldown expiry and raise. I should also expose a `forget_cache_failures()` helper for tests and deployments to reset state, and confirm the half-open behavior works \u2014 after cooldown expires, one retry is allowed through, and if it succeeds the breaker resets cleanly. I still need to double check account_page's handling of CacheUnreachable and any existing tests that assume look_up is called on every request.\n\n",
